Z||000000||count_iblis||10-27-2000||04:07 PM||||WAS THERE A 5 SPEED 85 GT? ||||reg||5
Z||000001||count_iblis||10-27-2000||04:08 PM||||ALSO DIDNT IT HAVE THE EXTRA GAUGE CLUSTER? ||||reg||1
Z||000002||2xsess||10-27-2000||04:10 PM||||I know for a fact that the 85GT didn't have the extra gauges. As for the 5spd, I don't think they made the 5spd until 86.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>85GT||||reg||1
Z||000003||count_iblis||10-27-2000||05:02 PM||||so what all would be needed to hook up the extra guage cluster?  Or is it possible?||||reg||5
Z||000004||2xsess||10-27-2000||06:40 PM||||Do a search on "aux gauges"<P>I sent you the page via email<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>85GT<p>[This message has been edited by 2xsess (edited 10-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||10-27-2000||06:47 PM||||all u need r the gages and cover, the wiring harness is already in the center dash for them.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||10-27-2000||06:56 PM||||Not on an '85, it isn't.  I have an '85 GT that I installed aux gauges in.  I only wished it had been a plug-n-play.  I had to find the correct wires and tap into them.||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||10-27-2000||09:07 PM||||The 5 speed Getrag became a Fiero option mid year in 1986. Fiero GT fastbacks with the 5 speed are sometimes called 1986-1/2. The early 1986 manual shift GT cars were 4 speeds.<P>Monkey is right. I've also done this ralley gauge install, plus I've wrote up an article to help others. Go to this link for more info.<P> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000001/HTML/20000815-2-001538.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000001/HTML/20000815-2-001538.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Pontiaddict||10-27-2000||09:33 PM||||There was no early 86 GT. All 86 GTs were 86.5.||||reg||1
Z||000009||DaRkLoRD||10-28-2000||12:15 AM||||Batboy's aux gauge article is on my website, in the mods section..<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000010||batboy||10-28-2000||07:42 AM||||Someone help me out here. I'm fairly sure there were early 1986 GT fastbacks made. There is a persistant rumor that says there were some 1986 notchback GT Fieros made, but that has been pretty much determined to be false, I think. Maybe I'm going senile, but I thought we had a couple Forum members with early 86 GTs?||||reg||1
Z||000011||batboy||10-28-2000||07:45 AM||||Darky, my article on your site looks great, but I wish we had a direct link to go right to that page.||||reg||1
Z||000012||stimpy||10-28-2000||10:59 AM||||Ahh, the old 86 GT rumor...<BR> I do have an early production run 86 GT.  It does have the 4 sp and also an obscure option for GT that was only available in early 86, the luggage rack.  <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=258191&a=1921599&p=16712376&Sequence=0">  <BR>However, the car is still considered an 86 1/2 because the body style itself was not introduced until February of 86, with all other 86 models having been introduced in Oct. of 85.  Again I will use the 64 Mustang comparison, as well as the 70 Camaro.  Neither of these models were ready at the introduction of the respective manufacturers launches, therefore they received midyear designations upon introduction. <BR>Once again, the midyear designation on 86 GT's have zero, nuthin, nada, zilch to do wth equipment or option availability. ALL 86 GT's are 86 1/2.  <P>------------------<BR>Loud Pipes Save Lives!      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/stimpy_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||batboy||10-28-2000||12:03 PM||||I guess that clear that up. At least I was correct about the early GT cars having 4 speeds and the later ones having 5 speeds...unless you have an auto, of course.||||reg||1
